I ran into an interesting behavior in TypeORM while updating a user’s password in NestJS.
@BeforeInsert()
async changeRootUserPassword(id: string, newPassword: string): Promise {
user.password = newPassword;
const savedUser = await user.save();
return !!savedUser?.id;
Result: The password is saved in plain text!
Scenario 2 – Password Hashed ✅
@BeforeInsert()
async changeRootUserPassword(id: string, newPassword: string): Promise {
user.password = newPassword;
const savedUser = await user.save();
return !!savedUser?.id;
Result: Password is properly hashed ✅
💡 Key Takeaway:
 When relying on TypeORM entity hooks like @BeforeUpdate(), ensure at least one other field changes — otherwise, your hook may not run, and important logic (like password hashing) can fail silently.
